      "section": "references",
      "text": "10 Due to the potential for our specialty sorption media to be scalable and versatile, the media may find their niche in full-scale PFAS removal applications including the remediation of contaminated surface waters, groundwaters, and landfill leachate. Adsorption is a diverse treatment that can be implemented as primary water treatment or as a pretreat - ment for further water polishing, thus contributing to engineering pro - cess design. For instance, Islam et al. [ 69 ] explored the complementary role of adsorption using green sorption media and nanofiltration to enhance the removal of both short- and long-chain PFAS while reducing membrane fouling. Where in a hybrid process, CPS and ZIPGEM in a column study were used as preliminary treatment for nanofiltration, demonstrating the contribution of the specialty adsorbents in membrane design by resulting in \u205316 % more long-chain PFAS removal than nanofiltration alone. This is also demonstrated by the treatment capacity and lifespan of the media, specifically for PFOA, recalling that it reached exhaustion in comparison to PFOS. For instance, the LUB and BC of CPS were 68.63 cm and 53.76 cm, respectively, indicating that approximately 75 % of the bed capacity was unused at the time of breakthrough with a C b of 5.0 ng \u22c5 g \u0000 1 ."
