      "section": "abstract",
      "text": "[ 20 ], and reduction mineralization [ 21 ]. These technologies are limited by their high cost and the risk of formation of intermediate by-products that may be more toxic than their parent molecules. Shorter-chain perfluoroalkyl carboxylates that contain 2 \u2013 6 carbons are driven by their high toxicity [ 22 ], while they are also more persistent and mobile and less degradable than the long-chain PFAS [ 15 ]. Specialty adsorbents are innovative sorption media with immense potential in water treatment applications due to their low cost, scalable treatment capacity, minimal maintenance, and prolonged service life. These sorption media can remove a variety of pollutants including nu - trients, metals, algal toxins, organic acids, pharmaceuticals, and other emerging contaminants simultaneously from aqueous solutions [ 23 \u2013 27 ]. In this study, the efficiencies of 2 recently developed adsorp - tion media in removing long- and short-chain PFAS from water were evaluated, and the removal mechanisms were investigated. These spe - cialty adsorbents are Zero-Valent-Iron and Perlite-based Green Envi - ronmental Media (ZIPGEM) and Clay-Perlite and Sand sorption media (CPS). Whereas the ZIPGEM media mix comprises sand, clay, perlite, and zero-valent \u2011 iron (ZVI), CPS contains sand, clay, and perlite, mixed at specific ratios."
